{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"eventual consistency eventual consistency","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md","fileName":"Eventual Consistency.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Eventual Consistency","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-21 22:12\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[CQRS and Ledger Data Architecture]] [[Event Sourcing and Projections]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["CQRS and Ledger Data Architecture","Event Sourcing and Projections","Projection","Read Model","Projection Checkpoint","hands-ondesignpatternswithcandnetcore.pdf","hands-ondomain-drivendesignwithnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"eventual consistency eventual consistency","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md","fileName":"Eventual Consistency.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Eventual Consistency","heading":"Eventual Consistency","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Eventual consistency means that a read model may temporarily lag behind committed source changes but is expected to converge after updates propagate. In a split command-and-query system, a successful write can precede the corresponding read projection, so a user may briefly see the old value. The application must make that delay acceptable through workflow design, status reporting, or a stronger consistency path for operations that cannot tolerate stale reads.\n\nIn an event-sourced system, the event stream is updated before asynchronous [[Projection]]s consume the new event. The consistency delay is therefore the interval between appending the event and updating every affected [[Read Model]]. Reliable subscriptions, retries, and [[Projection Checkpoint]]s make convergence possible, while the interface must not promise immediate visibility it cannot provide.","tags":["baby"],"links":["CQRS and Ledger Data Architecture","Event Sourcing and Projections","Projection","Read Model","Projection Checkpoint","hands-ondesignpatternswithcandnetcore.pdf","hands-ondomain-drivendesignwithnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"eventual consistency eventual consistency","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Eventual Consistency.md","fileName":"Eventual Consistency.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Eventual Consistency","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[hands-ondesignpatternswithcandnetcore.pdf]]\n[[hands-ondomain-drivendesignwithnetcore.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["CQRS and Ledger Data Architecture","Event Sourcing and Projections","Projection","Read Model","Projection Checkpoint","hands-ondesignpatternswithcandnetcore.pdf","hands-ondomain-drivendesignwithnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}